// The same policy with the forbid dropped and a broader permit added -- the shape a careless edit
// takes. It lets the entire internet connect on port 22.
//
// WHAT THE BUILT-IN CHECKS SAY ABOUT IT is the interesting part, and it is not "nothing". They
// report:
//
//     REDUNDANT permit #1   it fires, but another permit always would too
//
// which is true: `port == 22 && origin == "local"` is subsumed by `port == 22`. And the advice
// that follows from it -- delete the redundant rule -- makes the policy smaller and leaves the
// hole exactly where it was. The redundancy is a SYMPTOM of the over-broad permit, and a check
// that cannot know what the policy was for cannot tell you which of the two rules is the mistake.
//
// `firewall.tla` can, because it was told: it names the request that breaks the claim.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Connect",
    resource
)
when { context.input.port == 22 && context.input.origin == "local" };

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Connect",
    resource
)
when { context.input.port == 22 };
